{"text": "Category D is Physical Disability or Chronic Health Impairment. The educational description is: A student whose movement, mobility, musculoskeletal condition, or chronic health impairment significantly affects educational functioning and creates a need for ongoing inclusive education services. This description is about educational impact and the support required for meaningful participation. It should not be used as a prediction of intelligence, personality, or future potential.\n\nOur fictional student is Noah Williams, who is in Grade 7 and uses he/him pronouns. Noah has cerebral palsy and epilepsy. He uses a power wheelchair and eye-gaze AAC; his understanding is at grade level, but motor output and health needs affect access and pace. This background information matters because it connects the designation to observable access needs at school.\n\nWhen we plan for Noah, we ask three practical questions: What can the student already do? What barriers are interfering with participation or learning? And what support will remove those barriers while building independence? Those questions keep the profile individualized and useful. Planning should include the student, family, teacher, case manager, and relevant specialists.\n\nI will now hand the presentation to Presenter 2, who will begin with Noah’s strengths and communication.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-D-02.mp3", "words": [{"text": "Category", "start": 0.1, "end": 0.7, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 8}, {"text": "D", "start": 0.7125, "end": 1.1, "start_char": 9, "end_char": 10}, {"text": "is", "start": 1.1125, "end": 1.35, "start_char": 11, "end_char": 13}, {"text": "Physical", "start": 1.3625, "end": 1.7875, "start_char": 14, "end_char": 22}, {"text": "Disability", "start": 1.8, "end": 2.6, "start_char": 23, "end_char": 33}, {"text": "or", "start": 2.6125, "end": 2.8125, "start_char": 34, "end_char": 36}, {"text": "Chronic", "start": 2.825, "end": 3.2375, "start_char": 37, 